WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.375
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.693 --> 00:00:07.860
MARILIA MELO: Hi, everybody.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.410
My name is Marilia Melo.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:14.000
I'll be hosting this Fireside
Chat with Melissa and Andrew.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.930
And to start, I actually want to
ask you all who are here today,

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:22.820
how many of you do already have
an SRE team in your company?

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:24.514
Please raise your hand.

00:00:24.514 --> 00:00:25.593
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:25.593 --> 00:00:28.010
MELISSA BINDE: I see all the
Googlers raising their hands.

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:29.725
Stop that!

00:00:29.725 --> 00:00:31.100
MARILIA MELO: And
how many of you

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.190
are actually thinking about
starting a new SRE team

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:38.930
or hiring for your own,
already existing SRE team?

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:39.540
OK.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:40.040
Cool.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.310
So we'll be talking here today
about how to start an SRE

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:47.360
and also how to hire
SREs and other questions.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.580
This is open to questions
from you as well.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:52.910
I do have a few questions
that I will be starting with.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:56.690
But we will have time for you
to make questions as well.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:58.640
So please start
thinking about that.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.440
If I don't cover the questions
here, please raise your hand.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.710
And you're going to give
you the mic as well.

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:10.020
So the first question
I have on here--

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.040
it is probably a
challenging one--

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:17.150
is how do I know when my
org needs an SRE team?

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:20.420
So what justifies
for me an SRE team?

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:21.830
What issues exist?

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:24.857
And what are the
potential benefits?

00:01:24.857 --> 00:01:26.190
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: Want to start?

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:27.023
MELISSA BINDE: Sure.

00:01:27.023 --> 00:01:31.540
So the question is, how do I
know when I need an SRE team?

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:33.310
Andrew and I decided
ahead of time,

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:36.640
I would take the big company and
he would take the small company

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.890
perspective, so you'll get both.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.130
From a big company
perspective, what

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:44.740
we're looking at is, if
you have an SRE team,

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:47.230
how do you know when to
make an additional SRE team

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.780
or shard off to a new org?

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.850
And there's two
things we look at.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:56.720
You're looking for technology
and focus that is similar.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.250
So I was involved in
the creation of GCP SRE.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:04.000
And we split it off from our
internal tech infra-SRE teams.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.250
And we did that because we
saw that there was actually

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:08.919
a critical difference
between interacting

00:02:08.919 --> 00:02:10.840
with external customers
and the concerns

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:14.080
that external customers had
about how we handle their SLOs,

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:17.080
how we handled our responses,
that differed from how

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:18.940
we did internal software.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.720
And trying to combine
the two in one focus just

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.220
wasn't actually giving us the
amount of leadership bandwidth

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:25.490
that we needed.

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:30.040
So splitting that off helped us
service you folks much better

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:31.732
and allowed our
internal team to really

00:02:31.732 --> 00:02:33.190
be able to focus
on what they cared

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:36.720
most about, which was the
capacity, the efficiency focus.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.580
The other thing to do is to
look at your dev partners.

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:41.650
You want to have an
organization generally

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:44.510
that aligns at least
somewhat with them.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:48.872
And when you have one focus
covering a large dev swath,

00:02:48.872 --> 00:02:50.830
they may not be getting
the kind of partnership

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:51.663
they're looking for.

00:02:51.663 --> 00:02:53.122
So that was the
other thing that we

00:02:53.122 --> 00:02:54.880
did, was we were
able to then split up

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:59.260
our dev VPs across
the two of us and get

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.570
better coordination
with the dev teams,

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:03.520
better depth of
knowledge, and better

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:05.297
alignment with the business.

00:03:05.297 --> 00:03:07.880
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: So I'm going
to take more of the historical,

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:09.970
like how would you
seed your first SRE

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:12.190
team from Google's basis.

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:15.040
A show of hands here-- how
many people have gone from

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.428
and are currently at from 0
to 1 SRE teams, or just about

00:03:19.428 --> 00:03:22.750
to go to their first?

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:26.860
In Google's history, SRE was
formed because there was--

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:30.088
out of all of the broad, general
software engineering worlds,

00:03:30.088 --> 00:03:32.870
there were several people
who had self-identified

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:35.350
and clustered around
the production boundary,

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:36.580
the production interface.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.830
They were literally
called "production team."

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:41.700
And it was interesting because
it wasn't the production ops

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:42.700
team or what have you.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:46.550
It was the software engineers
most production-curious,

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:47.050
as it were.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:50.459
And I think it was
a very important

00:03:50.459 --> 00:03:53.381
and, I think, accidentally,
insightful thing that Google

00:03:53.381 --> 00:03:55.820
did which was, they said
we recognize this boundary

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:57.550
and we want to support it.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:03:58.500
So take a look around.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:03:59.890
Humans are curious creatures.

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:03.430
And they'll tend to just do what
they want to do, within reason.

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:06.390
See if you can
encourage and seed--

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:10.900
may I suggest-- an SRE team
from the general, traditional

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:15.000
engineering side with those
most production-adjacent,

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.940
in order to keep that
engineering aspect

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:20.303
from day one.

00:04:20.303 --> 00:04:21.720
MELISSA BINDE: I
often describe it

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.660
as, you're looking for the
folks who are more interested

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:27.015
in the activities
around the software

00:04:27.015 --> 00:04:29.880
than in chewing out
lines of code themselves.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:31.920
And I suspect this applies
to a bunch of folks

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:33.390
here because you're all here.

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:35.173
But the people who get
really annoyed when

00:04:35.173 --> 00:04:37.090
you have a bunch of bugs
that are out of date,

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:40.210
or your CI/CD pipeline
isn't working right,

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:41.990
or your pushes keep stalling.

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:44.140
And it's not that you
don't like writing code

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:45.910
but you keep being
attracted to solve

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:48.160
those problems
around the software,

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:49.960
not just in the software.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.680
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: So maybe
an indicative sign of this

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:56.300
is, like we were saying,
is if you are here,

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:00.760
how many of your colleagues at
any level of your organization

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:02.600
would also like to
be here right now?

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:04.910
Is it more than two?

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:07.380
You probably have a nascent
SRE team already happening.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.143
You should just fund it.

00:05:09.143 --> 00:05:10.060
MARILIA MELO: Awesome.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:12.130
I think we started
to talk a little bit

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:13.540
about the next question as well.

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:15.970
So we have two or three people--

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:19.540
n plus 2-- people that
want to do this SRE thing.

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:21.400
So we have an SRE team.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.340
It's a buzz word
recently, right?

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:24.070
SRE, SRE.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:26.140
So we created an SRE team.

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:29.110
But we're not really sure
what it should be doing.

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:32.530
So in reality, what
is the SRE team doing?

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:36.370
And we can either split and
start out versus big as well.

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:39.242
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: OK, so
side-note-- forgive me

00:05:39.242 --> 00:05:40.950
if I'm all
hyperbole-laden and come

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:42.158
to Candy Mountain about this.

00:05:42.158 --> 00:05:45.200
I spend a lot of my time doing
college and industry recruiting

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:46.510
for SREs to come to Google.

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:48.920
So naturally I'm biased
and excited about this.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:51.050
But the way I try to
explain the role of an SRE

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:52.198
is, it's really--

00:05:52.198 --> 00:05:54.740
whether or not you buy into the
whole left-brain, right-brain

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:55.240
thing.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:57.650
I'm going to instead say,
two halves of the brain

00:05:57.650 --> 00:05:59.437
are proactive and
reactive, right?

00:05:59.437 --> 00:06:01.270
Yes, of course, there's
the carry-the-pager,

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:05.060
the react-to-various-things,
the vigilance and the diligence

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:06.290
type stuff.

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:07.560
This is all well and good.

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:11.540
But there's also this very
much a always-be-prepared,

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:15.550
within reason, within
proportionate reason sort

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:16.050
of way.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:20.130
What I mean is, having
the regular conversations

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:21.570
in your team, the
disaster drills,

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:24.510
the let's think about
the best and worst

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:26.320
that can happen type stuff.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:27.720
So it's an equal balance.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.450
You want to be there at the
earliest part of the design

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:34.650
phase, in order to be able to
make the subtle improvements

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:36.210
from the experience
and the gravitas

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:37.850
that you and SRE
has had for years

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:40.718
from doing this sort of thing,
to influence and make sure

00:06:40.718 --> 00:06:43.080
that the arrow bars on a
launch of something that

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:47.340
has been designed x months
ago aren't very large.

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:49.380
The biggest non-event
of the summer

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:52.140
should be the launch of
said product or project

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:53.430
that you were involved in.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:58.500
So I'm looking for folks who
are good engineers, but also

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:00.710
good reverse engineers,
which is something sometimes

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:01.810
we forget to think about.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:06.700
I'm not necessarily saying it
should be a bunch of IT jockeys

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:09.522
who question authority
and always look

00:07:09.522 --> 00:07:11.980
to break the alarm clocks open
to figure out how they work,

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:13.090
or any variant of that.

00:07:13.090 --> 00:07:15.340
What I'm thinking about is
people who are inquisitive,

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:17.070
people who are
analytical, people

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:18.810
who want to understand
a whole system.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:22.410
And that can come from either
software engineering, and/or IT

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:23.050
and ops.

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:25.890
It's a spectrum.

00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:29.710
These are the sorts of things
I look for in the fellow folks

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:31.657
that I bring into
an SRE organization.

00:07:31.657 --> 00:07:33.490
MELISSA BINDE: And I
want to expand on that.

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:35.860
It is dangerously
easy to develop

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.740
a monoculture in
your SRE team based

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:41.410
on the values of the person
who is most influential,

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:44.120
which might be the start or the
first manager or the leader,

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:44.820
whatever.

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:47.620
If that person is highly
biased towards, very often,

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:50.620
reactive stuff, you
can get an SRE team

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:52.660
that is going to
double down way too

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:56.140
much on being excited by
incidents and way too little

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:58.100
on long-term planning.

00:07:58.100 --> 00:07:59.500
If the person
happens to actually

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:01.540
be like a frustrated
software engineer who'd

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:03.520
be rather writing
code that got told

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:04.420
they're not good
enough so they have

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:06.080
to be an SRE-- by the
way, that's terrible.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:06.490
Don't do that.

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:07.360
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: Horrible.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:08.943
MELISSA BINDE: But
you're going to get

00:08:08.943 --> 00:08:10.690
a team that is
constantly railing

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:13.930
against wanting to do
features on the product

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:15.940
instead of doing
automation code.

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:19.180
So a really critical thing
is to think through all the

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:19.680
different--

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:23.237
SRE is an incredibly
multidisciplinary activity.

00:08:23.237 --> 00:08:25.570
You need people who are good
at deep software debugging.

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:27.820
You need people who are good
at understanding distributed

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:28.270
systems.

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:30.145
You need people who are
good at understanding

00:08:30.145 --> 00:08:32.392
the ways your
infrastructure can go wrong.

00:08:32.392 --> 00:08:34.059
There's so many
different aspects of it.

00:08:34.059 --> 00:08:35.601
Because then you've
got the immediate

00:08:35.601 --> 00:08:37.720
versus the long-term, the
break fix, the designs.

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:40.630
You want as diverse
intellectually a crew

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:41.682
as you can.

00:08:41.682 --> 00:08:44.140
If you're interviewing someone
who is completely unlike you

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:46.570
but interested in it,
that's a really good sign.

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:48.670
And focusing on that
when building your team,

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:50.660
drawing from as
diverse a background,

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:53.590
and diverse in every
possible way that you can,

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:55.900
you will build a much
stronger team at the end.

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:58.450
Because everyone can learn
how to answer a pager.

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:00.700
But having people who are
interested in the long term,

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:02.730
people who are interested
in the short term.

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:06.220
We have some SREs who their
most exciting moment is

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:07.960
when we declare a
Google-wide incident,

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:10.880
and they get to be at the
command of the helm of getting

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:12.880
a whole bunch of people
to respond to something.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:13.922
I've watched those folks.

00:09:13.922 --> 00:09:15.490
I'm not those people.

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:17.410
My interest is really
on the product side.

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:20.212
And how do I make customers
as happy as we possibly can?

00:09:20.212 --> 00:09:21.670
And then I have
some colleagues who

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:24.410
are so deep into the hardware
and the optimizations

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:27.640
and what performance
can we eke out of this.

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:30.470
But all of us together
are what make SRE great.

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:34.480
Any one over-indexed would
not produce a good team.

00:09:37.583 --> 00:09:39.250
MARILIA MELO: In this
respect, how do we

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:41.110
actually prioritize
the activities

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:42.590
that the SRE should be doing?

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:46.540
For example, should we be
focusing on emergency response?

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:50.499
Should we be focusing on the
dashboards, the monitoring?

00:09:50.499 --> 00:09:52.832
How is that conversation like
to focus on the activities

00:09:52.832 --> 00:09:54.215
that they would be doing?

00:09:54.215 --> 00:09:55.673
ANDREW WIDDOWSON:
I think it really

00:09:55.673 --> 00:09:58.200
depends on the size and
the trajectory of the team.

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:02.382
Because a very well tenured,
fully fleshed out team,

00:10:02.382 --> 00:10:04.590
I think, will have a different
resting set of ratios.

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:07.007
And also I don't think that
every single person on the SRE

00:10:07.007 --> 00:10:10.080
team will have the exact
same set of interests.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:11.980
So you afford the
luxury of being

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:14.680
able to kind of load-balance
from within, once

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:18.010
you get to a series of stable
engagements, a relatively

00:10:18.010 --> 00:10:20.280
well-known space, but
still a lot of change

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:22.840
in the velocity of the
individual products.

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:24.670
When you start--
and, by the way,

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:27.160
I have my own set of opinions
on the minimum spanning

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:29.530
set of SREs.

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:31.225
Best SRE [INAUDIBLE]
that I've ever seen

00:10:31.225 --> 00:10:32.685
is we're going to
hire one person.

00:10:32.685 --> 00:10:34.060
We're going to
work all the SWEs.

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:36.320
That's called a scapegoat
reliability engineer.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:38.100
And that's how you
do your SRE work.

00:10:40.953 --> 00:10:42.860
We can go more into
what not to do later.

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:45.100
But essentially, let's say you
have a minimum set of people

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:46.642
who are all very
new to the role, who

00:10:46.642 --> 00:10:48.570
are trying to get up to speed.

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:51.580
There's a lot of running to get
in front of the treadmill slash

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:54.210
people mover that may happen.

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:57.108
But it's how you do it, I
think, that's most important,

00:10:57.108 --> 00:11:00.600
that will afford you the
ability to get to a point

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:03.070
like I mentioned previously.

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:04.810
MARILIA MELO: Do
you want to add--

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:06.435
how do you prioritize
the activities

00:11:06.435 --> 00:11:07.560
the SRE team will be doing?

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:10.060
MELISSA BINDE: I don't have
much to add to what Andrew said,

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:12.790
other than to answer a slightly
different aspect of it,

00:11:12.790 --> 00:11:15.630
which is that I would simply
encourage you to make sure you

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:18.450
are actually prioritizing.

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:21.510
Because it's very
easy to not prioritize

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:26.400
and to have the urgent
always trump the important.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:29.250
One of the ways I like to
talk about this is in terms

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:34.207
of budgets, where you say, OK,
we are planning on having 20%

00:11:34.207 --> 00:11:36.540
of our team's energy going
towards working with the devs

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:40.740
on new products, 50% of our
team's energy going towards

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:43.170
doing automation on the stuff
we've already on-boarded

00:11:43.170 --> 00:11:44.150
and-- math--

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:48.870
30% of our team's energy going
towards operational issues.

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:50.970
And then actually
test that that's true.

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:53.477
And when it's not,
stop and think--

00:11:53.477 --> 00:11:55.560
is this really actually
the thing to do or are you

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:57.040
just reacting?

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:00.240
I have SRE teams tell me they
can't possibly tell their devs

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:04.340
that they cannot spend only 20%
of their time on new products.

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:06.060
I'm like, well, have you tried?

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:08.580
Because it actually turns out
to work surprisingly well.

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:09.890
People understand the
concept of budgets.

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:11.723
They understand I have
a lot of things to do

00:12:11.723 --> 00:12:13.830
and this is how I'm
prioritizing things.

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:16.410
So that's one of my favorite
ways to handle is with budgets.

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:17.030
MARILIA MELO: Nice.

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:18.530
And you talk a
little bit about what

00:12:18.530 --> 00:12:20.550
I'm going to ask next again.

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:21.854
Thank you.

00:12:21.854 --> 00:12:25.920
So how should the SRE teams
view their relationships

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:28.775
with the developers
in the company?

00:12:28.775 --> 00:12:30.400
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: I
think it's a matter

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:33.560
of mutual respect at all times.

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:36.540
So for example, here's a
couple of anti-patterns.

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:39.512
I'm going to come in,
and you've got velocity

00:12:39.512 --> 00:12:41.220
and you've got reliability.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:43.890
And they're 180 degrees out of
phase with each other, right?

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:44.390
Wrong.

00:12:44.390 --> 00:12:45.120
But OK.

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:48.040
So what's the best way to
increase our stability?

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:49.460
Fire all the humans.

00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:50.400
Stop making changes.

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:50.900
Right?

00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:53.550
Like, that is the best way
to quickly reduce yourselves

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:56.567
to the exaggeration of a
strictly only ops organization.

00:12:56.567 --> 00:12:58.650
MELISSA BINDE: It's amazing
how stable your system

00:12:58.650 --> 00:12:59.927
is over the winter holidays.

00:12:59.927 --> 00:13:01.260
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: Yeah, exactly.

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:02.552
Let's just freeze all the time.

00:13:02.552 --> 00:13:06.390
So the way I like to pitch the
value of SRE to any dev team

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:09.540
that I work with is, we want to
be there from day one with you.

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:12.810
We've got your back
and vice-versa.

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:16.140
I also want to explain, like the
way I talk with my hands a lot.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:19.500
I try to make it
maximally orthogonal,

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:25.510
the relationship between
velocity and stability.

00:13:25.510 --> 00:13:29.020
How many people here learned
to drive an automobile

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:32.380
in a car that had a driver's ed
person in the passenger seat?

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:36.316
And they had a brake.

00:13:36.316 --> 00:13:40.498
If your dev organization
perceives you

00:13:40.498 --> 00:13:43.081
as the driver ed
instructor with the brake,

00:13:43.081 --> 00:13:44.700
you're doing it wrong.

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:48.400
If they perceive you as the
weird person riding shotgun

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:51.883
with you that has an
extra accelerator pedal,

00:13:51.883 --> 00:13:54.362
you're probably
doing it way better.

00:13:54.362 --> 00:13:55.820
I will often work
with my dev teams

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:57.530
to say, how can we go faster?

00:13:57.530 --> 00:13:59.060
I'm going to write
the automation.

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:01.480
I'm going to write the
safe guardrails to make it

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:04.647
so that we can decouple some
of the scariness from this.

00:14:04.647 --> 00:14:05.980
We'll make things auto-rollback.

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:07.647
We'll figure out ways
to do experiments.

00:14:07.647 --> 00:14:10.750
We'll segment stuff.

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:13.400
One cherry-picked CR will
not tank the whole release.

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:18.058
We'll be able to march forward
and backward on our features,

00:14:18.058 --> 00:14:20.478
promoting the most reliable
ones, those sorts of things.

00:14:20.478 --> 00:14:23.382
So if you get into
a shouting match,

00:14:23.382 --> 00:14:24.834
if you get into a postmortem--

00:14:24.834 --> 00:14:26.334
I'm going to point
at someone I know

00:14:26.334 --> 00:14:29.190
in the audience-- it's a blame
exercise-- they can take it.

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:31.150
That's bad.

00:14:31.150 --> 00:14:34.320
It really should be, I've saved
your butt several times over.

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:37.780
And we've made this
faster together.

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:39.390
We've saved each other's butts.

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:40.980
I think that's really good.

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:43.080
If you start off from an
adversarial relationship,

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:46.380
there is no way, in my opinion--
from having looked at this time

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:49.866
and time again-- to somehow--
it's not like you can flip

00:14:49.866 --> 00:14:52.120
like binary bit overflow,
and suddenly you're

00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:53.500
back around to respect.

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:55.947
You have to start from,
we're here together.

00:14:55.947 --> 00:14:57.530
And sometimes that
comes structurally,

00:14:57.530 --> 00:14:59.680
like you were forged
from the same fire,

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:01.620
but from different VPs.

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:04.730
It's very rarely from,
someone screwed up

00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:06.290
and now we're going
to replace them

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:08.588
with the people who are
mystical, and magical,

00:15:08.588 --> 00:15:12.074
and misunderstood or the
people who are adversarial.

00:15:12.074 --> 00:15:13.766
There's really a
set of fine balance.

00:15:13.766 --> 00:15:16.058
And if you don't check in
all the time and communicate,

00:15:16.058 --> 00:15:19.670
that will go off the
rails just automatically.

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:21.670
MELISSA BINDE: There's a
couple of failure modes

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:25.180
I've seen that SREs can
take deep offense to and not

00:15:25.180 --> 00:15:28.990
realize that it's just the
devs not always thinking things

00:15:28.990 --> 00:15:30.580
through.

00:15:30.580 --> 00:15:32.428
Probably the prime one is--

00:15:32.428 --> 00:15:34.720
just going off what you said--
the end is that SREs are

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:36.480
magical unicorns--

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:38.500
unicorns.

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:40.300
In terms of the
devs-- like I've had

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:43.060
a dev say this to me, that
they couldn't possibly

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:44.300
take on this project.

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:45.298
And I said, why not?

00:15:45.298 --> 00:15:47.590
They said, well, we don't
know anything about the code.

00:15:47.590 --> 00:15:50.740
I'm like, literally, what
do you do for a job, dude?

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:52.960
I mean, I'm sorry.

00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:54.860
We take on code we
don't know all the time.

00:15:54.860 --> 00:15:55.360
So can you.

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:01.240
It is easy for an
SRE team to fall

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:02.950
into kind of a blame
game and a pointing

00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:04.367
there and say that
all the devs do

00:16:04.367 --> 00:16:05.800
is throw stuff over the wall.

00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:07.420
You have to sit
down and tell them

00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:10.520
when that's happening and
say, hey, this isn't OK.

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:13.540
Can we talk about this?

00:16:13.540 --> 00:16:16.990
Decently often, you're
having an SRE team

00:16:16.990 --> 00:16:19.120
evolving out of an ops team.

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:20.410
And you have a lot of habits.

00:16:20.410 --> 00:16:22.510
You have a lot of
things you're accepting.

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:24.732
And it can be very
dangerous for us

00:16:24.732 --> 00:16:29.040
when we take a very adversarial
stance, as Andrew mentioned,

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:32.598
from day one, as opposed to
sitting down and working out

00:16:32.598 --> 00:16:34.890
some new relationships, and
then having some agreements

00:16:34.890 --> 00:16:36.700
that, hey, when things
go off the rails,

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:37.908
we're going to talk about it.

00:16:37.908 --> 00:16:39.390
We're going to fix it.

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:41.370
I had one other thing
I was going to say.

00:16:41.370 --> 00:16:43.545
What was it?

00:16:43.545 --> 00:16:45.420
MARILIA MELO: I can
actually ask specifically

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:46.390
to your experience.

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:48.590
Because when you
started GCP SRE,

00:16:48.590 --> 00:16:51.932
the devs were
already there, right?

00:16:51.932 --> 00:16:54.330
You brought in the SREs.

00:16:54.330 --> 00:16:56.610
Is there any specific
things that you

00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:59.412
did to make sure that
the SREs and devs--

00:16:59.412 --> 00:17:00.204
MELISSA BINDE: Yes.

00:17:00.204 --> 00:17:01.037
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:17:01.037 --> 00:17:04.319
I'd change that very slightly,
which is that we actually

00:17:04.319 --> 00:17:05.819
hired a lot of--

00:17:05.819 --> 00:17:08.369
GCP has grown so
incredibly fast--

00:17:08.369 --> 00:17:10.290
thank you all for buying it--

00:17:10.290 --> 00:17:13.680
that we have hired a
large number of executives

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:15.240
from outside Google.

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:17.447
So in areas of Google that
have grown more slowly,

00:17:17.447 --> 00:17:19.271
it's a very organic--

00:17:19.271 --> 00:17:22.170
the VPs get beaten into
shape by their peers.

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:24.480
And they cooperate a
little bit more with SRE.

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:28.079
That is less true in GCP.

00:17:28.079 --> 00:17:31.430
My job for a while was
literally educator of VPs.

00:17:31.430 --> 00:17:37.090
And there was this class of
VPs that I taught all of them.

00:17:37.090 --> 00:17:40.560
One of the key things
that we have, though,

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:42.450
in our toolbox at
Google that I think

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:44.730
isn't always common
outside, that

00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:47.670
helps with this
adversarial relationship,

00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:52.920
is the ability to politely
and calmly walk away,

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:55.110
the handing back of the pager.

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:57.480
And that's something that
I get people asking me.

00:17:57.480 --> 00:18:00.847
Like I'll go into customer
meetings and the head of ops

00:18:00.847 --> 00:18:02.430
will pull me aside
and go, no, really,

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:04.140
can you actually do that?

00:18:04.140 --> 00:18:05.190
Like have you?

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:07.850
I've actually--
not me personally--

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:09.840
but I have had a
conversation with devs.

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:13.470
I've had one conversation where
I said, your service is bad.

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:14.810
And I am losing SREs.

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:16.950
And I'm not going to
block their transfer.

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:19.500
When this team gets too low,
we're going to have no choice.

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:22.020
Would you like to
change course now?

00:18:22.020 --> 00:18:23.745
Spoiler-- they did.

00:18:23.745 --> 00:18:25.620
And I've had other teams
where we've sat down

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:27.660
and we've had like
months-long conversation of,

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:29.040
is this a healthy relationship?

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:31.860
Is this something
we should continue?

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:35.520
And the thing is that
having that position to take

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:38.640
is both a relief valve
for SRE, because you

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:41.670
don't need to drive it to
the point of adversarialness.

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:44.190
But it's also a really good
sort of early warning discussion

00:18:44.190 --> 00:18:45.590
with your devs.

00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:49.980
But to have that you need a
culture where it's OK for devs

00:18:49.980 --> 00:18:51.170
to be on call.

00:18:51.170 --> 00:18:55.638
And that is a very hard
conversation in some companies.

00:18:55.638 --> 00:18:57.180
Depending where you
come from, that's

00:18:57.180 --> 00:19:00.210
either natural or
incredibly insane.

00:19:00.210 --> 00:19:03.330
Feel free to tell them that
at Google all the devs, even

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:05.790
the ones with SREs,
are actually expected

00:19:05.790 --> 00:19:07.950
to be able to be on call.

00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:08.490
So we do.

00:19:08.490 --> 00:19:09.510
We do do shared on-call.

00:19:09.510 --> 00:19:11.700
Like most of my teams, the
devs will answer pagers

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:12.370
during the day.

00:19:12.370 --> 00:19:14.790
And SRE takes them
overnight and after hours.

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:17.740
So, yeah, you can use my name.

00:19:17.740 --> 00:19:19.800
Because often, well,
Google doesn't.

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:21.440
No, we do.

00:19:21.440 --> 00:19:25.560
But you need those escape valves
because, at the end of the day,

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:28.390
as much as we want
to say partnership,

00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:30.990
just like how QA tends to get
the short end of the stick

00:19:30.990 --> 00:19:33.780
because they're the end
of the development cycle,

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:36.300
SRE can very easily get
the short end of the stick

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:37.710
if you have not
put in some rails

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:39.085
to help with the
power imbalance.

00:19:39.085 --> 00:19:40.502
ANDREW WIDDOWSON:
May I underscore

00:19:40.502 --> 00:19:41.670
this particular phenomenon.

00:19:41.670 --> 00:19:43.860
And it is of course a
thought exercise to you

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:46.630
as to how this may or may not
apply at your own company.

00:19:46.630 --> 00:19:50.700
At Google, let's just say
that there are about 60,000

00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:52.310
engineer-shaped people.

00:19:52.310 --> 00:19:55.460
There are 2,886--
as of yesterday--

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:57.190
SREs at the company.

00:19:57.190 --> 00:19:59.210
So think about that ratio--

00:19:59.210 --> 00:20:01.710
2 point mumble to 60.

00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:03.800
So SRE are a scarce resource.

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:06.030
We're really hard to hire for.

00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:08.860
That also means we go
where the action is.

00:20:08.860 --> 00:20:12.450
And so, working backwards
from that, at Google,

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:16.860
most software engineers on most
projects that are not Search,

00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:20.145
Ads, Chrome Apps, et cetera,
or the cream of the crop

00:20:20.145 --> 00:20:21.800
revenue-wise thereof--

00:20:21.800 --> 00:20:23.040
MELISSA BINDE: GCP.

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:26.390
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: GCP--
forgive me, sorry.

00:20:26.390 --> 00:20:28.102
GCP happened after my time.

00:20:28.102 --> 00:20:28.602
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:28.602 --> 00:20:29.554
Oh, hello, everyone.

00:20:33.370 --> 00:20:38.530
Most things that launch at
Google never get an SRE.

00:20:38.530 --> 00:20:40.270
Most things that launch
at Google that do

00:20:40.270 --> 00:20:42.490
get an SRE don't get--

00:20:42.490 --> 00:20:44.625
an SRE team, mind you.

00:20:44.625 --> 00:20:50.200
A two-continents, minimum of six
people on each team SRE team.

00:20:50.200 --> 00:20:53.780
Some of them, it will
take quarters or years.

00:20:53.780 --> 00:20:58.030
And so by the time SRE gets to
the regular, stable, on-call

00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:02.170
vet, golly gosh, it's a really
well maintainable system.

00:21:02.170 --> 00:21:03.820
Because the people
who are in charge

00:21:03.820 --> 00:21:06.610
of adding features, and
potentially operational burden

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:09.000
to it, have had to deal with it.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.818
So on the flip side--

00:21:11.818 --> 00:21:13.610
I hate to use the word
leverage, because it

00:21:13.610 --> 00:21:15.830
sounds like I'm spouting out
some "Harvard Business Review"

00:21:15.830 --> 00:21:16.420
nonsense.

00:21:16.420 --> 00:21:21.920
But the real leverage here
is if your ops team already

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:26.528
has 100% of the on-call,
where can they go from there?

00:21:26.528 --> 00:21:28.820
And that may be what a lot
of people are thinking here.

00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:30.680
Here's a real quick one.

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:32.460
Maybe not all new
launches abruptly,

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:36.740
but the mid-tier or
long-tail launches

00:21:36.740 --> 00:21:38.246
don't have SREs on them.

00:21:38.246 --> 00:21:39.990
And that's not meant
to be punitive.

00:21:39.990 --> 00:21:42.430
That's meant to be,
we're focusing this--

00:21:42.430 --> 00:21:45.110
again, at the risk
of mysticism or SRE

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:49.230
are this weird thing--
no, that's bad too.

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:51.320
SRE being attached
to a project being

00:21:51.320 --> 00:21:53.040
there are, we're here to help.

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:54.160
This is a sign of focus.

00:21:54.160 --> 00:21:57.090
This is a sign of
enthusiasm, of skill

00:21:57.090 --> 00:21:58.830
above and beyond
that is necessary.

00:21:58.830 --> 00:22:01.800
So you want to work with
SREs, as opposed to,

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:03.400
I want to add one more project.

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:04.980
What's one more
project, the Toad

00:22:04.980 --> 00:22:07.680
says, as we add more
pagers to the ops team.

00:22:07.680 --> 00:22:09.430
That's so incredibly different.

00:22:09.430 --> 00:22:11.330
And you can iterate
towards that,

00:22:11.330 --> 00:22:17.017
while maintaining that
leverage, respect, scarcity.

00:22:17.017 --> 00:22:19.100
MELISSA BINDE: And if
you're thinking about that--

00:22:19.100 --> 00:22:21.500
I did see enough nods that
I'm going to do this quick

00:22:21.500 --> 00:22:22.540
follow-up--

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:25.100
realize that the deeper in
your stack something is,

00:22:25.100 --> 00:22:28.610
paradoxically, the more
important is to have SRE on it.

00:22:28.610 --> 00:22:31.310
Your leaf node, the stuff
that your customers actually

00:22:31.310 --> 00:22:33.960
interact with, that's
probably pretty simple stuff.

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:37.350
And when it goes down, it
doesn't affect other things.

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:41.180
Your database, your Pub/Sub
services, your everything else

00:22:41.180 --> 00:22:42.905
in that big
infrastructure layer,

00:22:42.905 --> 00:22:44.030
that's where the danger is.

00:22:44.030 --> 00:22:46.113
Because the blast radius
when those things go out,

00:22:46.113 --> 00:22:48.930
and the complexity when those
things go out, is much higher.

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:50.790
So a good place
to start is where

00:22:50.790 --> 00:22:54.650
if you have devs launching a new
service or a new feature that's

00:22:54.650 --> 00:22:58.190
gaining adoption, that is
a good place to say, hey,

00:22:58.190 --> 00:23:00.590
why don't you guys support
that until it gets big?

00:23:00.590 --> 00:23:02.010
And we're happy to help you.

00:23:02.010 --> 00:23:03.860
We're happy to figure
out how to improve

00:23:03.860 --> 00:23:05.840
it, what monitoring you need.

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:08.348
But why don't you guys
handle it at the start?

00:23:08.348 --> 00:23:10.890
MARILIA MELO: So we talked about
how the relationship of SREs

00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:12.070
and devs are.

00:23:12.070 --> 00:23:13.700
And we also started
talking about how

00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:16.760
the relationship of SREs and the
business owners, or the product

00:23:16.760 --> 00:23:18.490
team, because
they're the ones that

00:23:18.490 --> 00:23:21.127
are actually going to decide
where the SREs are going.

00:23:21.127 --> 00:23:21.710
Is that right?

00:23:24.615 --> 00:23:25.990
ANDREW WIDDOWSON:
So the question

00:23:25.990 --> 00:23:31.290
is towards how should SRE
relate with product deciders?

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:35.980
As opposed to maybe feature
engineering designers?

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:39.660
MARILIA MELO: How do the
relationship between the teams

00:23:39.660 --> 00:23:42.460
that need SREs and how
do they request that

00:23:42.460 --> 00:23:43.338
to the product team?

00:23:43.338 --> 00:23:44.130
MELISSA BINDE: Yes.

00:23:44.130 --> 00:23:46.560
So that is one of the
other things that we do.

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:49.280
Because as Andrew
said, we don't want

00:23:49.280 --> 00:23:53.462
SREs to be-- so it turns
out, the way humans work

00:23:53.462 --> 00:23:55.170
is that if I give you
something for free,

00:23:55.170 --> 00:23:56.620
you do not care at all about it.

00:23:56.620 --> 00:23:59.700
And if I charge you a penny, you
suddenly care way more than one

00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:01.350
penny's worth.

00:24:01.350 --> 00:24:03.727
And so we actually
treat SREs similarly.

00:24:03.727 --> 00:24:05.310
When you're looking
at your headcount,

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:07.740
your headcount as
a VP is the sum

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:10.680
of the SREs on your product,
plus your engineers.

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:12.820
SREs come out of the same pool.

00:24:12.820 --> 00:24:15.570
So if you're not willing
to staff SRE enough,

00:24:15.570 --> 00:24:17.570
SRE will not take
on your service.

00:24:17.570 --> 00:24:20.940
But when you do, that
counts against your product.

00:24:20.940 --> 00:24:23.820
And that puts your
skin in the game.

00:24:23.820 --> 00:24:25.440
You are literally
and deliberately

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:26.878
trading off engineers--

00:24:26.878 --> 00:24:28.420
ANDREW WIDDOWSON:
Software engineers.

00:24:28.420 --> 00:24:30.628
MELISSA BINDE: --software
engineers-- caught myself--

00:24:30.628 --> 00:24:34.350
software engineers for
SREs very deliberately.

00:24:34.350 --> 00:24:37.680
And while at Google we do report
through a separate management

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:41.380
chains that we have the ability
to escalate and to say no--

00:24:41.380 --> 00:24:44.070
which we also
think is critical--

00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:46.560
that's a very tight partnership.

00:24:46.560 --> 00:24:49.040
And what part of your
question did I miss?

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:50.700
Maybe Andrew can
cover what I missed.

00:24:50.700 --> 00:24:54.300
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: I think the
mutual exclusivity of staffing.

00:24:54.300 --> 00:24:56.550
What do you mean I'm melting
down a dev to get an SRE?

00:24:56.550 --> 00:24:59.030
If only it was
fungible like that--

00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:03.118
is actually really,
I think, important.

00:25:03.118 --> 00:25:04.160
Other than that, really--

00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:06.077
MELISSA BINDE: Oh, I
remember I wanted to say.

00:25:06.077 --> 00:25:07.530
Sorry, Andrew.

00:25:07.530 --> 00:25:13.270
SREs should be doing work that
SREs uniquely can do the best.

00:25:13.270 --> 00:25:15.090
So if your SREs are
writing features

00:25:15.090 --> 00:25:18.240
for your dev product,
that's not something SREs

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:19.450
can uniquely do the best.

00:25:19.450 --> 00:25:22.600
The idea is that you're
staffing appropriately.

00:25:22.600 --> 00:25:25.752
So your SRE team is the size
to get the most out of that.

00:25:25.752 --> 00:25:27.960
And if you actually move
that back into the dev team,

00:25:27.960 --> 00:25:30.500
you probably have to
put more people on it.

00:25:30.500 --> 00:25:33.377
But simultaneously, if you put
too much headcount into the SRE

00:25:33.377 --> 00:25:35.960
team, such that they're flowing
over and writing dev features,

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:37.290
they're going to be
less efficient in that,

00:25:37.290 --> 00:25:38.990
because that's not their
specialization, that's

00:25:38.990 --> 00:25:39.962
not their passion.

00:25:39.962 --> 00:25:41.670
ANDREW WIDDOWSON:
Though that being said,

00:25:41.670 --> 00:25:43.750
encouraging
cross-pollination rotations,

00:25:43.750 --> 00:25:47.432
like being able to say I'm the
grand poobah of dollar sign

00:25:47.432 --> 00:25:52.072
company, I hereby
pre-authorize that anyone with

00:25:52.072 --> 00:25:56.280
a quarter's notice can spend
six months going to either team

00:25:56.280 --> 00:25:58.500
from either team, in
order to build empathy--

00:25:58.500 --> 00:26:01.640
shocking, right-- and breadth--

00:26:01.640 --> 00:26:02.300
amazing.

00:26:02.300 --> 00:26:04.383
MELISSA BINDE: We actually
financially incentivize

00:26:04.383 --> 00:26:06.108
our devs to spend
six months with SRE.

00:26:06.108 --> 00:26:06.900
And it's brilliant.

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:10.007
And an awfully large number
of them convert to SRE.

00:26:10.007 --> 00:26:11.340
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: More than 50%.

00:26:11.340 --> 00:26:14.580
So it's billed as a catch
and release program.

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:18.200
But it's actually more
of a Hotel California.

00:26:18.200 --> 00:26:19.970
They want to stay with
us, which is great.

00:26:19.970 --> 00:26:21.470
MARILIA MELO: So
from all your talk,

00:26:21.470 --> 00:26:23.660
it seems like SREs are
not only taking the pager

00:26:23.660 --> 00:26:24.910
and looking at monitors.

00:26:24.910 --> 00:26:27.890
It seems there is a lot of
culture related to that.

00:26:27.890 --> 00:26:32.100
And then you have an interesting
title as SRE Culture Lead.

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:33.140
What does that mean?

00:26:33.140 --> 00:26:35.010
What does an SRE
culture involve?

00:26:35.010 --> 00:26:35.690
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: I don't
know what it means either.

00:26:35.690 --> 00:26:36.780
But I'm about to find out.

00:26:36.780 --> 00:26:37.540
No.

00:26:37.540 --> 00:26:41.350
So I spent 12 years on-call
for all the different facets

00:26:41.350 --> 00:26:44.470
of Google Search, which was
a fascinating and wild ride.

00:26:44.470 --> 00:26:45.520
And then for the--

00:26:45.520 --> 00:26:47.478
co-current with that--
for the last four years,

00:26:47.478 --> 00:26:49.690
up until basically
a month ago, I

00:26:49.690 --> 00:26:53.830
was also the co-founder and
a pretty significant designer

00:26:53.830 --> 00:26:56.700
of a lot of the orientation
programs for all new SREs.

00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:57.700
So I've done the hiring.

00:26:57.700 --> 00:26:59.590
I've done the training.

00:26:59.590 --> 00:27:02.410
I've done the
panicked one-on-ones

00:27:02.410 --> 00:27:04.600
in the night, career
existential angst.

00:27:04.600 --> 00:27:08.420
I've done it all with many
people in the SRE organization.

00:27:08.420 --> 00:27:14.390
And I think SRE as a
concept, and Google SRE

00:27:14.390 --> 00:27:17.900
as it is applied, has some
really fascinating things

00:27:17.900 --> 00:27:18.400
to it.

00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:20.192
I think there are things
we do really well.

00:27:20.192 --> 00:27:24.310
But in our company, there
are 240-something SRE teams.

00:27:24.310 --> 00:27:27.290
Divide by 2, because we're
paired over continents.

00:27:27.290 --> 00:27:29.470
That's a lot of different
ways of rolling the dice

00:27:29.470 --> 00:27:32.137
and trying out different ways of
doing things-- even just things

00:27:32.137 --> 00:27:34.990
like weekly disaster
drills, or how

00:27:34.990 --> 00:27:38.140
every single dev-SRE
partnership is designed.

00:27:38.140 --> 00:27:39.220
I can give you an answer.

00:27:39.220 --> 00:27:40.670
And it might be representative.

00:27:40.670 --> 00:27:43.299
But chances are, there's
a lot of variance.

00:27:43.299 --> 00:27:45.632
So I'm actually going around
and visiting teams one week

00:27:45.632 --> 00:27:49.690
at a time to cross-pollinate
a lot of this stuff.

00:27:49.690 --> 00:27:51.510
I think a lot of it is--

00:27:51.510 --> 00:27:54.480
to me, to typify the
SRE personality--

00:27:54.480 --> 00:27:56.910
I won't, because there are
multiple personalities--

00:27:56.910 --> 00:28:03.230
but the set of personalities
is inquisitiveness, the ability

00:28:03.230 --> 00:28:06.938
to circumvent hierarchy in
order to put the user first.

00:28:06.938 --> 00:28:08.480
That sounds like a
lot of corp speak.

00:28:08.480 --> 00:28:10.278
Let me try this again.

00:28:10.278 --> 00:28:12.254
Realizing where the
rubber meets the road,

00:28:12.254 --> 00:28:15.712
that you may be the most
decisive person in the room,

00:28:15.712 --> 00:28:18.973
and then encouraging
people to take that on.

00:28:18.973 --> 00:28:21.390
Case in point-- what I have
told every single new SRE that

00:28:21.390 --> 00:28:24.180
has started at our company
is, when you are on call

00:28:24.180 --> 00:28:27.430
and you get paged, you
are hereby officially

00:28:27.430 --> 00:28:31.577
VP worth of authority for
your product in the moment.

00:28:31.577 --> 00:28:33.910
Because, don't worry, it's
only several thousand dollars

00:28:33.910 --> 00:28:35.590
a second.

00:28:35.590 --> 00:28:40.250
We give you great
responsibility and trust.

00:28:40.250 --> 00:28:43.110
SRE is a mesh, not
a tree, when it

00:28:43.110 --> 00:28:46.290
comes to solving our systems,
because our systems are

00:28:46.290 --> 00:28:47.670
distributed systems.

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:49.560
Our systems aren't
pointy-haired boss

00:28:49.560 --> 00:28:52.562
under pointy-haired boss under
pointy-haired boss trees.

00:28:52.562 --> 00:28:55.300
No offense.

00:28:55.300 --> 00:28:58.495
So we need folks who
can think like a mesh.

00:29:01.160 --> 00:29:02.650
It all comes into that.

00:29:02.650 --> 00:29:04.750
And so I think our
culture is trust

00:29:04.750 --> 00:29:07.190
each other, support each other.

00:29:07.190 --> 00:29:11.820
It's not all hold hands and
kumbayah, but it's radial,

00:29:11.820 --> 00:29:12.450
it's mesh.

00:29:12.450 --> 00:29:14.640
It's not a tree, both
technologically and

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:16.213
people-wise.

00:29:16.213 --> 00:29:18.630
MELISSA BINDE: I want to expand
on that just a little bit.

00:29:18.630 --> 00:29:18.940
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: Please.

00:29:18.940 --> 00:29:21.190
MELISSA BINDE: Andrew,
as always, is spot on.

00:29:21.190 --> 00:29:22.730
And I have little to say.

00:29:22.730 --> 00:29:24.830
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: Too kind.

00:29:24.830 --> 00:29:27.150
MELISSA BINDE: When I was
considering coming to Google,

00:29:27.150 --> 00:29:31.700
I had a friend who is the
director of all of our network

00:29:31.700 --> 00:29:35.430
and traffic stuff, everything.

00:29:35.430 --> 00:29:37.920
I was utterly
amazed that he went

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:41.610
on-call and utterly
terrified of the prospect.

00:29:41.610 --> 00:29:43.170
And could not
believe it, thought

00:29:43.170 --> 00:29:45.180
he was like god among men.

00:29:45.180 --> 00:29:46.530
And I started at Google.

00:29:46.530 --> 00:29:49.903
And I went through one of
the very early SRE edus,

00:29:49.903 --> 00:29:50.820
which is where I met--

00:29:50.820 --> 00:29:52.305
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: I'm
glad I didn't damage you.

00:29:52.305 --> 00:29:53.580
Everything worked out great.

00:29:53.580 --> 00:29:56.220
MELISSA BINDE: And I saw
the level of support,

00:29:56.220 --> 00:29:58.350
the level of tooling,
the level of camaraderie,

00:29:58.350 --> 00:30:01.152
and the level of
trust and acceptance.

00:30:01.152 --> 00:30:03.360
And I told him I had no more
respect for him anymore,

00:30:03.360 --> 00:30:06.960
because I saw how
much help he had.

00:30:06.960 --> 00:30:09.960
But it actually took a lot
of the scary and the mystery

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:10.660
out of it.

00:30:10.660 --> 00:30:14.670
And one of the things I value
most about SRE at Google--

00:30:14.670 --> 00:30:17.180
because I work with
a lot of dev teams--

00:30:17.180 --> 00:30:21.670
we are probably one of the
most diverse orgs I work with.

00:30:21.670 --> 00:30:23.700
And I think that's
because to work here,

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:26.890
you have to be open to new
ideas-- to work in SRE,

00:30:26.890 --> 00:30:28.530
you have to be
open to new ideas,

00:30:28.530 --> 00:30:30.020
to different ways of thinking.

00:30:30.020 --> 00:30:31.950
And you have to be able
to support each other

00:30:31.950 --> 00:30:33.210
and work together.

00:30:33.210 --> 00:30:34.967
Blameless postmortems
only work when

00:30:34.967 --> 00:30:37.300
you can actually pull off
blameless with a straight face

00:30:37.300 --> 00:30:38.890
and mean it.

00:30:38.890 --> 00:30:42.340
And to have a culture to
enforce that kind of thing,

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:47.500
you really need to keep on
it and to not tolerate people

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:49.480
who are going to perturb that.

00:30:49.480 --> 00:30:53.620
You need to be much more
careful caretakers of not hiring

00:30:53.620 --> 00:30:55.690
jerks, of getting rid
of people who can't

00:30:55.690 --> 00:30:57.220
work with people like them.

00:30:57.220 --> 00:31:02.860
Because this is a high
stress role once in a while.

00:31:02.860 --> 00:31:05.020
And then you're
not on-call again.

00:31:05.020 --> 00:31:07.420
But every moment of
on-call could explode

00:31:07.420 --> 00:31:09.400
into Google.com is down.

00:31:09.400 --> 00:31:11.810
And no one wants to be the
reason that Search is down--

00:31:11.810 --> 00:31:13.720
although Andrew, didn't
you take Search down once?

00:31:13.720 --> 00:31:14.200
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: I did.

00:31:14.200 --> 00:31:15.116
MELISSA BINDE: Yes, excellent.

00:31:15.116 --> 00:31:17.074
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: And my
team bought a bag of--

00:31:17.074 --> 00:31:19.086
did you know, for
$100, you can get

00:31:19.086 --> 00:31:22.570
a shredded, de-circulated
pile of $100,000 US

00:31:22.570 --> 00:31:24.010
from the Treasury Department?

00:31:24.010 --> 00:31:27.560
And my team once awarded me
with a bag of shredded money

00:31:27.560 --> 00:31:29.410
with a Post-it Note on
it, with the location

00:31:29.410 --> 00:31:32.470
of the cluster that I had
borked rather considerably.

00:31:32.470 --> 00:31:34.660
And that was when I knew I
was one of the cool kids.

00:31:34.660 --> 00:31:36.348
Because I wrote the postmortem.

00:31:36.348 --> 00:31:38.140
We found out some really
interesting things

00:31:38.140 --> 00:31:40.840
about the actions that I did
and the ramifications thereof

00:31:40.840 --> 00:31:45.045
that we now use as part of our
proper reactive procedures.

00:31:45.045 --> 00:31:46.670
I was like, maybe
this will work-- bam.

00:31:46.670 --> 00:31:48.320
Oh, it made it worse.

00:31:48.320 --> 00:31:50.060
But now we know, right?

00:31:50.060 --> 00:31:52.310
And that would
never have happened

00:31:52.310 --> 00:31:57.540
on many other types of dev and
IT ops type of relationships.

00:31:57.540 --> 00:32:00.350
MELISSA BINDE: So think
about the type of environment

00:32:00.350 --> 00:32:02.240
you need to build for
that to be actually

00:32:02.240 --> 00:32:04.070
true to everyone's core.

00:32:04.070 --> 00:32:06.440
That, to me, is
what SRE culture is.

00:32:06.440 --> 00:32:08.780
And that's the only
way you can survive.

00:32:08.780 --> 00:32:11.570
Because if you have hiding,
if you have finger pointing,

00:32:11.570 --> 00:32:13.790
if you have jerks
on your team, that's

00:32:13.790 --> 00:32:15.817
going to come out some
time and hit prod.

00:32:15.817 --> 00:32:17.650
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: Or
that you'll take it out

00:32:17.650 --> 00:32:18.860
on your devs in the same way.

00:32:18.860 --> 00:32:20.068
You'll just start projecting.

00:32:20.068 --> 00:32:21.985
MARILIA MELO: So I have
only one more question

00:32:21.985 --> 00:32:23.150
before we open the mic.

00:32:23.150 --> 00:32:26.290
So please prepare
your questions.

00:32:26.290 --> 00:32:28.240
And maybe it's a
challenging one--

00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:33.247
but how do you define
success of an SRE team?

00:32:33.247 --> 00:32:35.330
MELISSA BINDE: I had a
really good answer for this

00:32:35.330 --> 00:32:36.163
like 44 minutes ago.

00:32:36.163 --> 00:32:37.997
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: Well,
I want to underscore

00:32:37.997 --> 00:32:40.090
a point you made earlier
around, if you were

00:32:40.090 --> 00:32:42.870
to release the headcount
behind the SRE team

00:32:42.870 --> 00:32:45.470
back to the SWE team, that it
would-- the software engineer

00:32:45.470 --> 00:32:46.860
team-- that it would take more.

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:48.712
There's a sense
of, we are working

00:32:48.712 --> 00:32:50.920
on the most important things
at the hot, gooey center

00:32:50.920 --> 00:32:52.240
of our product.

00:32:52.240 --> 00:32:54.310
We are where the
rubber meets the road.

00:32:54.310 --> 00:32:59.860
We are at the interface of
the system and the user.

00:32:59.860 --> 00:33:02.710
How many people here have seen
"Tron," the original movie?

00:33:02.710 --> 00:33:03.390
That's Tron.

00:33:03.390 --> 00:33:04.830
They fight for the user.

00:33:04.830 --> 00:33:06.300
SRE, Tron, very similar.

00:33:06.300 --> 00:33:07.550
MELISSA BINDE: I am a manager.

00:33:07.550 --> 00:33:09.650
So I have a little bit
more quantitative answer.

00:33:09.650 --> 00:33:12.510
But you can pick
from between us.

00:33:12.510 --> 00:33:16.090
For me it's that your SLOs--

00:33:16.090 --> 00:33:17.550
if you don't know
what an SLO is,

00:33:17.550 --> 00:33:19.980
please talk to
someone from CRE--

00:33:19.980 --> 00:33:23.010
that your SLOs actually
are user-centric,

00:33:23.010 --> 00:33:25.570
that your SLOs actually
reflect the user experience,

00:33:25.570 --> 00:33:27.240
not what you were
able to measure,

00:33:27.240 --> 00:33:31.260
not what was easy, that your
team is spending at least half

00:33:31.260 --> 00:33:33.810
the time doing enduring work.

00:33:33.810 --> 00:33:35.970
That doesn't necessarily
mean writing code.

00:33:35.970 --> 00:33:37.740
But it means you're
doing work that

00:33:37.740 --> 00:33:39.750
is going to continue to
provide value at least

00:33:39.750 --> 00:33:41.560
six months into the future.

00:33:41.560 --> 00:33:43.470
That's my best definition
of engineering work

00:33:43.470 --> 00:33:45.010
that I can come up with.

00:33:45.010 --> 00:33:48.315
I think it's really critical
that you're not just patching

00:33:48.315 --> 00:33:49.440
things and calling it code.

00:33:49.440 --> 00:33:51.760
Look, it's not toil,
because I wrote code.

00:33:51.760 --> 00:33:55.260
Well, if it's throw-away
work, it's still not enduring.

00:33:55.260 --> 00:33:58.020
And that you have an appropriate
relationship with your dev

00:33:58.020 --> 00:33:58.680
team.

00:33:58.680 --> 00:34:01.997
It is possible to be too
chummy with your dev team.

00:34:01.997 --> 00:34:04.080
I don't know if you guys
know the phrase "captured

00:34:04.080 --> 00:34:05.360
regulators."

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:07.980
But I've seen SREs
who will bend over

00:34:07.980 --> 00:34:10.020
backwards to justify
ignoring the fact

00:34:10.020 --> 00:34:11.880
that they went out
of error budget,

00:34:11.880 --> 00:34:16.219
because they've forgotten
that part of their job is--

00:34:16.219 --> 00:34:20.580
I like to describe it as you are
the insurance policy that you

00:34:20.580 --> 00:34:23.460
took out for yourself
for the future

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:25.889
when you were about to
make a bad decision.

00:34:25.889 --> 00:34:28.560
And that's what things like
error budgets and SRE help for.

00:34:28.560 --> 00:34:31.230
We say, hey, in the
calm times we all

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:33.630
agreed we would freeze
in this situation.

00:34:33.630 --> 00:34:35.739
And now we really want
to get this release out.

00:34:35.739 --> 00:34:36.989
We know the customers want it.

00:34:36.989 --> 00:34:38.239
But we're out of error budget.

00:34:38.239 --> 00:34:39.429
And, and, and, and--

00:34:39.429 --> 00:34:41.889
well, what do the
customers think?

00:34:41.889 --> 00:34:43.949
Do they actually value
this more than the risk

00:34:43.949 --> 00:34:45.719
of additional outages?

00:34:45.719 --> 00:34:49.020
And so it is very easy for
SREs to get caught up in that.

00:34:49.020 --> 00:34:51.630
Because you want a good
relationship with the dev team.

00:34:51.630 --> 00:34:56.040
But you also need a foot outside
the role at the same time.

00:34:56.040 --> 00:34:59.420
And obviously, bad would
be being the other way.

00:34:59.420 --> 00:35:02.610
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: To this
point, another thought exercise.

00:35:02.610 --> 00:35:04.560
I talked about scarcity.

00:35:04.560 --> 00:35:08.130
To me, the successful
SRE team is the SRE team

00:35:08.130 --> 00:35:10.620
that every dev team wishes
they could work with.

00:35:10.620 --> 00:35:12.295
Not because it's,
here's a black hole

00:35:12.295 --> 00:35:13.980
I can throw my
pagers into, hahaha.

00:35:13.980 --> 00:35:15.810
Because in that
case, if you were

00:35:15.810 --> 00:35:17.593
to say at your
company gathering,

00:35:17.593 --> 00:35:19.260
raise your hand if
you want an SRE team.

00:35:19.260 --> 00:35:21.090
And if everyone raises their
hand and snickers a little bit,

00:35:21.090 --> 00:35:22.350
you know that's not good.

00:35:22.350 --> 00:35:24.370
So here's an exercise.

00:35:24.370 --> 00:35:26.470
Maybe this will work
for you, maybe it won't.

00:35:26.470 --> 00:35:29.850
To your dev teams, ask
if you had an SRE team

00:35:29.850 --> 00:35:33.760
that you were working with,
what would you work on together?

00:35:33.760 --> 00:35:37.040
And if the answer is all
the bad stuff-- ehnn.

00:35:37.040 --> 00:35:40.120
It's this interface to
apply for the honor of being

00:35:40.120 --> 00:35:41.370
able to work with an SRE team.

00:35:41.370 --> 00:35:42.640
It's the weirdest of things.

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:45.660
It's again to the consumer
electronics marketing technique

00:35:45.660 --> 00:35:49.470
of $0.01 costs so
much more than $0.00.

00:35:49.470 --> 00:35:53.460
But then also on the flip side,
right, take all of the answers

00:35:53.460 --> 00:35:55.960
to this ingestion
survey and take it

00:35:55.960 --> 00:36:00.000
to your fledgling new SRE team
and say, which of these answers

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:01.920
do you think is actually right?

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:03.670
And if the SRE
team then says, oh,

00:36:03.670 --> 00:36:05.920
the ones that said we get
to work on tickets all day--

00:36:05.920 --> 00:36:06.780
brrrn.

00:36:06.780 --> 00:36:07.893
That's also bad, right?

00:36:07.893 --> 00:36:09.810
It should probably be
somewhere in the middle.

00:36:09.810 --> 00:36:14.970
Where if you as the arbiter of
who gets staffed to what says,

00:36:14.970 --> 00:36:18.690
I can see that we want to reward
the right sets of matches,

00:36:18.690 --> 00:36:21.080
where the SRE team says,
we want to extend and make

00:36:21.080 --> 00:36:23.280
this product launch faster.

00:36:23.280 --> 00:36:27.152
We want to stay up less
at night for all of us.

00:36:27.152 --> 00:36:29.265
And if the dev team
says, we'd really

00:36:29.265 --> 00:36:32.880
like to get some hard
critique of our systems

00:36:32.880 --> 00:36:35.270
so that we don't launch
in a fiery ball of doom

00:36:35.270 --> 00:36:37.100
later, all of that is good.

00:36:37.100 --> 00:36:40.058
But you can imagine the cross
product of all of the bad.

00:36:40.058 --> 00:36:40.850
Maybe try that out.

00:36:40.850 --> 00:36:42.053
See what happens.

00:36:42.053 --> 00:36:43.470
MELISSA BINDE: Can
I add one thing

00:36:43.470 --> 00:36:44.590
that's not what you asked?

00:36:44.590 --> 00:36:45.240
MARILIA MELO: Of course.

00:36:45.240 --> 00:36:46.050
MELISSA BINDE: OK.

00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:48.550
This is something I want to
make sure I say, just in case it

00:36:48.550 --> 00:36:50.750
doesn't come up in a question.

00:36:50.750 --> 00:36:54.440
I'm not a big fan of
methodologies that require

00:36:54.440 --> 00:36:57.060
100% adherence to be true.

00:36:57.060 --> 00:37:00.620
So any kind of purity
tests or whatever.

00:37:00.620 --> 00:37:05.180
And I have heard, when talking
with people, sometimes people

00:37:05.180 --> 00:37:09.010
see the SRE book as
essentially a purity test.

00:37:09.010 --> 00:37:10.760
You have to do all of
these things in this

00:37:10.760 --> 00:37:12.290
or you're not an SRE.

00:37:12.290 --> 00:37:14.160
And I love the story
that I will tell,

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:15.702
that I usually don't
tell to anywhere

00:37:15.702 --> 00:37:17.480
near this large an
audience, of an L6

00:37:17.480 --> 00:37:20.150
engineer who showed up at stand
up one day, right after the SRE

00:37:20.150 --> 00:37:20.960
book was released--

00:37:20.960 --> 00:37:22.670
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: L6
meaning relatively senior.

00:37:22.670 --> 00:37:24.378
MELISSA BINDE: I'm
sorry, staff engineer,

00:37:24.378 --> 00:37:25.970
like one level above senior.

00:37:25.970 --> 00:37:28.400
And he shows up with
the SRE book in hand.

00:37:28.400 --> 00:37:30.722
And he says, hey, guys, I
read this really cool book

00:37:30.722 --> 00:37:31.430
over the weekend.

00:37:31.430 --> 00:37:34.970
Maybe we should do
some things in it.

00:37:34.970 --> 00:37:38.490
Even at Google, every team
doesn't do everything.

00:37:38.490 --> 00:37:40.410
And we do things different ways.

00:37:40.410 --> 00:37:42.630
And we pick and we choose
what's important to us

00:37:42.630 --> 00:37:44.720
and what's important
to our service.

00:37:44.720 --> 00:37:48.470
The kind of one central
tenet that I see about SRE

00:37:48.470 --> 00:37:50.540
is the understanding
of incentives

00:37:50.540 --> 00:37:53.600
and the understanding of
the value of engineering.

00:37:53.600 --> 00:37:56.438
If you're understanding how you
and your dev team have aligned

00:37:56.438 --> 00:37:58.730
incentives and you're making
agreements ahead of time--

00:37:58.730 --> 00:38:00.058
whatever those are--

00:38:00.058 --> 00:38:02.600
and if you're making sure that
your team is doing engineering

00:38:02.600 --> 00:38:04.880
work, all of the rest
are things that you

00:38:04.880 --> 00:38:07.942
may use to get into that
place or to get better.

00:38:07.942 --> 00:38:09.650
But please don't think
that if you're not

00:38:09.650 --> 00:38:12.987
doing wheel of misfortune,
you're not doing SRE.

00:38:12.987 --> 00:38:14.570
Actually, the CRE
folks would probably

00:38:14.570 --> 00:38:17.000
tell you that error budgets and
SLOs have to be on that list.

00:38:17.000 --> 00:38:17.860
Yeah, David's here.

00:38:17.860 --> 00:38:19.640
Yeah, I'm getting
lots of nods from CRE.

00:38:19.640 --> 00:38:22.720
All right, add that
to the third one.

00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:24.380
Thank you, David.

00:38:24.380 --> 00:38:26.952
But you don't have
to do everything.

00:38:26.952 --> 00:38:28.910
You don't have to do
everything from the start.

00:38:28.910 --> 00:38:31.202
And pick and choose what
works for you and your company

00:38:31.202 --> 00:38:32.120
culture.

00:38:32.120 --> 00:38:33.230
It's a cool area.

00:38:33.230 --> 00:38:35.820
This is something unlike
anything else we do.

00:38:35.820 --> 00:38:37.610
And it's an opportunity
for those of us

00:38:37.610 --> 00:38:41.390
who love infrastructure and who
love the stuff around software,

00:38:41.390 --> 00:38:43.050
to be able to have
this as a career.

00:38:43.050 --> 00:38:44.810
And it's a very exciting time.

00:38:44.810 --> 00:38:48.840
ANDREW WIDDOWSON: And the word
asymptotic really applies here.

00:38:48.840 --> 00:38:51.200
You want to make
a large leap away

00:38:51.200 --> 00:38:56.630
from a traditional or stale
or practice of 10 years ago

00:38:56.630 --> 00:39:00.650
type situation of devs and ops.

00:39:00.650 --> 00:39:03.978
You want to make a large stride,
a very strong foray into that.

00:39:03.978 --> 00:39:05.770
And then ease off and
ease off and ease off

00:39:05.770 --> 00:39:08.245
as you get closer to some
sort of theoretical limit.

00:39:08.245 --> 00:39:10.065
Many companies I've
talked to have said,

00:39:10.065 --> 00:39:10.980
well, we're going to
try one little thing

00:39:10.980 --> 00:39:11.938
and see how that works.

00:39:11.938 --> 00:39:13.438
I'm going to be the
only person here

00:39:13.438 --> 00:39:15.682
who is going to tell you
that's not going to work.

00:39:15.682 --> 00:39:16.946
Everyone else here is going to
be like, yeah, come talk to us

00:39:16.946 --> 00:39:17.613
more about this.

00:39:17.613 --> 00:39:19.026
The first hit is free.

00:39:19.026 --> 00:39:20.984
If you say, we're going
to make this one little

00:39:20.984 --> 00:39:22.692
incremental thing,
it's not going to work

00:39:22.692 --> 00:39:24.840
and you're wasting your time.

00:39:24.840 --> 00:39:26.650
So I'll be the bad cop here.

00:39:26.650 --> 00:39:28.378
But now, for everyone
here who's nice

00:39:28.378 --> 00:39:30.420
cop, and would love to
tell you more about how we

00:39:30.420 --> 00:39:31.800
can work together to do this--

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:33.080
because I'm sure you
have lots of questions.

00:39:33.080 --> 00:39:35.130
And we're going to answer a
lot of them up here on stage.

00:39:35.130 --> 00:39:36.420
But for the extra
ones after this,

00:39:36.420 --> 00:39:38.378
will you please raise
your hand if you're a CRE

00:39:38.378 --> 00:39:40.292
or you're a dyed-in-the-wool
Google SRE here,

00:39:40.292 --> 00:39:41.750
who would love to
answer questions,

00:39:41.750 --> 00:39:44.450
look around and find the face of
the next person with their hand

00:39:44.450 --> 00:39:45.050
raised.

00:39:45.050 --> 00:39:48.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

